  "_id": "6682bd06ba0e94a3c4025a0b",
  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 1,
  "questionLabels": [],
  "question": {
    "text": "The number of species from the following in which the central atom uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math> hybrid orbitals in its bonding is _________.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>SiO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>BeCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "4",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo> </mo><mi>has</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math> hybridisation and angular shape due to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> lone pair of electrons.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mi>has</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi>sp</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo> </mo></mrow></msup></math> hybridised nitrogen atom and pyramidal shape due to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math> lone pair of electrons.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>SiO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mi>has</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo> </mo></math>hybridised atoms, it is a network solid and the shape is tetrahedral around each silicon atom. </p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mi>has</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> hybridised sulphur atom and the shape is V-shaped due to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math> lone pair of electrons.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mi>has</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> hybridised boron atom and the shape is trigonal planar.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BeCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><mi>sp</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><mi>sp</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math></p><p>Hence the answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math></p>",
    "image": null
  },
